<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="(1,4)-β-D-xylan degradation" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-6717" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Oryza sativa Japonica Group. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; |FRAME: Xylans Xylan| is found in the cell walls of plants and some green and red algae, and is the major constituent of |FRAME: Hemicelluloses|. The most common type of xylan is by far |FRAME: 1-4-beta-Xylan|, a linear polymer of |FRAME: BETA-D-XYLOSE| residues linked by (1&amp;rarr;4) glycosidic bonds. This polysaccharide backbone is often decorated by additional sugars, forming complex polymers such as |FRAME: Arabinoxylan arabinoxylan| and |FRAME: Glucuronoxylans glucuronoxylan|. Arabinoxylans have been identified in wheat, rye, barley, oat, rice, and sorghum, as well as in some less common plants, including pangola grass, bamboo shoots and rye grass. |FRAME: Glucuronoxylans Glucuronoxylans| and |FRAME: Glucuronoarabinoxylans glucuronoarabinoxylans| are located mainly in the secondary wall and function as an adhesive by forming covalent and non-covalent bonds with |FRAME: Lignins lignin|, |FRAME: CELLULOSE cellulose|, and other polymers essential to the integrity of the cell wall. Angiosperm (hardwood) glucuronoxylans also have a high rate of substitution (7080%) by acetyl groups, at position 2 and/or 3 of the &amp;beta;-D-xylopyranosyl. The degradation of cell wall is immensely important in industrial production as a supplement in animal feed, food and beverages, textiles, bleaching and production of biofuels. The main enzymes involved in xylan degradation are the xylanases. These enzymes are produced mainly by microorganisms that break down plant cell walls, but are also present in marine algae, protozoans, crustaceans, insects, snails and seeds of land plants |CITS: [9118232]|. The principle source of xylanases for the industry are filamentous fungi, since they secrete these enzymes into the medium and their xylanase levels are very high |CITS: [15944805]|. In recent years there has been a focus on xylanases from extremophiles that can withstand high temperatures and extreme pH found during industrial processes |CITS: [21120468]|. Due to the complex structure of xylan polymers, degradation requires a number of different types of enzymes working in tandem. These include endoxylanases (|FRAME: 3.2.1.8-RXN &quot;EC 3.2.1.8, endo-1,4-&amp;beta;-xylanase&quot;|; |FRAME: 3.2.1.136-RXN &quot;EC 3.2.1.136, glucuronoarabinoxylan endo-1,4-&amp;beta;-xylanase&quot;|), &amp;beta;-xylosidases (|FRAME: 3.2.1.156-RXN &quot;EC 3.2.1.156, oligosaccharide reducing-end xylanase&quot;|; |FRAME: 3.2.1.37-RXN &quot;EC 3.2.1.37, xylan 1,4-&amp;beta;-xylosidase&quot;|), ferulate esterases (|FRAME: 3.1.1.73-RXN &quot;EC 3.1.1.73&quot;|), acetylxylan esterases (|FRAME: 3.1.1.72-RXN &quot;EC 3.1.1.72&quot;|), &amp;alpha;-glucuronidases (|FRAME: 3.2.1.139-RXN &quot;EC 3.2.1.139&quot;|), arabinases (|FRAME: 3.2.1.99-RXN &quot;EC 3.2.1.99&quot;| and arabinofuranosidases (|FRAME: 3.2.1.55-RXN &quot;EC 3.2.1.55&quot;|).</Description>
